caps (6.3%) did much better."],["Interpretation","In every window the SAA underperformed simple benchmarks on both return and risk-adjusted return. The optimistic CMEs ranked EM as the most attractive asset, but history (2008–2017) rewarded US equities and high-quality bonds instead. The backtest shows how sensitive mean-variance optimisation is to return inputs: optimistic, forward-looking assumptions produced a concentrated portfolio that was not robust to the realised market regime. Notably, the SAA's realised 10-year volatility (13.7%) is almost exactly what the CME model expected, but its realised return was far lower."],["Recommendations","For an endowment client, consider (1) a cap on any single asset class, e.g. 30–35%, to reduce concentration risk; (2) blending optimistic with base-case and pessimistic CMEs, or using resampled or Black-Litterman methods; and (3) a higher strategic weight to US large caps and IG bonds. Remember that a backtest measures fit to the past, while the CMEs are 10-year forward-looking views, so past underperformance does not by itself invalidate the EM thesis."]]}